                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-102


                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  20F1'                         EXADDR:
  20F1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  20F2'   32 2761'              	STA	SRNGF
  20F5'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  20F6'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  20F8'   CA 2121'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  20FB'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  20FC'   CD 2136'              	CALL	EXNUM
  20FF'   DA 211F'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2102'   22 2762'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2105'   22 2764'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2108'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2109'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  210B'   C2 2118'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  210E'   13                    	INX	D
  210F'   CD 2136'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2112'   DA 211F'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2115'   22 2764'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2118'                         EXA_YS:
  2118'   EB                    	XCHG
  2119'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  211B'   32 2761'              	STA	SRNGF
  211E'   C9                    	RET
  211F'                         EXA_NO:
  211F'   EB                    	XCHG
  2120'   C9                    	RET
  2121'                         EXA_AL:
  2121'   23                    	INX	H
  2122'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2123'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2126'   22 2762'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2129'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  212C'   22 2764'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  212F'   E1                    	POP	H
  2130'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2132'   32 2761'              	STA	SRNGF
  2135'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  2136'                         EXNUM:
  2136'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2137'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2139'   D8                    	RC
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-103


  213A'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  213C'   3F                    	CMC
  213D'   D8                    	RC
  213E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2141'                         EXN_LP:
  2141'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2142'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2144'   DA 215E'              	JC	EXN_DN
  2147'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2149'   D2 215E'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  214C'   13                    	INX	D
  214D'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  214E'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  214F'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  2151'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2152'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  2153'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  2154'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  2155'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  2156'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  2157'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2158'   D1                    	POP	D
  2159'   09                    	DAD	B
  215A'   C1                    	POP	B
  215B'   C3 2141'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  215E'                         EXN_DN:
  215E'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  215F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  2160'                         EX_S:
  2160'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2161'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2162'   CA 21A4'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  2165'   32 2766'              	STA	SDELIM
  2168'   23                    	INX	H
  2169'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  216C'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  216F'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  2170'   CD 21AA'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2173'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2174'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2175'   CA 21A4'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  2178'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  217B'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  217D'   32 2731'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2180'   11 2773'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2183'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2185'   CD 21AA'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2188'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2189'   32 2772'              	STA	SREPLN
  218C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  218D'   32 2767'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2190'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2191'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-104


  2193'   C2 219C'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2196'   23                    	INX	H
  2197'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2199'   32 2767'              	STA	SGFLAG
  219C'                         EX_SG:
  219C'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  219D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  219E'   C2 1F73'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  21A1'   C3 21CA'              	JMP	SUBGO
  21A4'                         SUBNOP:
  21A4'   21 201F'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  21A7'   C3 1F81'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  21AA'                         SCOPY:
  21AA'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  21AC'                         SCP_LP:
  21AC'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  21AD'   B9                    	CMP	C
  21AE'   D2 21C7'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  21B1'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  21B2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21B3'   CA 21C7'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  21B6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  21B7'   21 2766'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  21BA'   BE                    	CMP	M
  21BB'   E1                    	POP	H
  21BC'   CA 21C6'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  21BF'   12                    	STAX	D
  21C0'   13                    	INX	D
  21C1'   23                    	INX	H
  21C2'   04                    	INR	B
  21C3'   C3 21AC'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  21C6'                         SCP_SK:
  21C6'   23                    	INX	H
  21C7'                         SCP_DN:
  21C7'   AF                    	XRA	A
  21C8'   12                    	STAX	D
  21C9'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  21CA'                         SUBGO:
  21CA'   CD 0ED3'              	CALL	GZONE
  21CD'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  21D0'   22 2770'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  21D3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  21D6'   22 276E'              	SHLD	SOFFS		;   search's SN_BAD does
  21D9'   AF                    	XRA	A
  21DA'   32 2768'              	STA	SANY
  21DD'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  21E0'   22 276A'              	SHLD	SBACK
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-105


  21E3'   3A 2761'              	LDA	SRNGF
  21E6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21E7'   CA 2210'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  21EA'   2A 2762'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  21ED'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  21EE'   B5                    	ORA	L
  21EF'   CA 228C'              	JZ	SUBBAD		; ':0s' -- there is no line 0
  21F2'   2A 2764'              	LHLD	SRNG2
  21F5'   EB                    	XCHG
  21F6'   2A 2762'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  21F9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; CY iff SRNG2 < SRNG1
  21FC'   DA 228C'              	JC	SUBBAD		; backwards: vim offers to swap, this refuses
  21FF'   EB                    	XCHG			; HL = the last line, DE = the first
  2200'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD
  2203'   23                    	INX	H		; HL = the lines in the range
  2204'   22 276C'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2207'   2A 2762'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  220A'   CD 0E44'              	CALL	GOLN
  220D'   C3 2216'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2210'                         SUB_1L:
  2210'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2213'   22 276C'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2216'                         SUBLP:
  2216'   CD 2292'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2219'   B7                    	ORA	A
  221A'   CA 2228'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  221D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  221F'   32 2768'              	STA	SANY
  2222'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2225'   22 276A'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2228'                         SUB_NX:
  2228'   2A 276C'              	LHLD	SLINES
  222B'   2B                    	DCX	H
  222C'   22 276C'              	SHLD	SLINES
  222F'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2230'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2231'   CA 224F'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  2234'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2237'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2238'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  223B'   3A 2768'              	LDA	SANY
  223E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  223F'   CA 2249'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  2242'   2A 276A'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2245'   23                    	INX	H
  2246'   22 276A'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2249'                         SUB_AE:
  2249'   CD 0989'              	CALL	ATEOF
  224C'   D2 2216'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  224F'                         SUBDON:
  224F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  2252'   3A 2768'              	LDA	SANY
  2255'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-106


  2256'   CA 2274'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  2259'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  225B'   32 26B2'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  225E'   2A 276A'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2261'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2262'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2263'   CA 226E'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  2266'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2268'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  226B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  226E'                         SUB_PL:
  226E'   CD 0EEB'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  2271'   C3 0700'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  2274'                         SUBNFD:
  2274'   2A 276E'              	LHLD	SOFFS
  2277'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  227A'   2A 2770'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  227D'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2280'   21 200B'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2283'   CD 1F81'              	CALL	EXERR
  2286'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2289'   C3 1F7C'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  228C'                         SUBBAD:
  228C'   21 1FF9'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  228F'   C3 1F76'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  2292'                         SUBLIN:
  2292'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2293'   32 2769'              	STA	SLCHG
  2296'   CD 2311'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2299'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  229C'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  229C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  229F'   DA 22A7'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  22A2'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  22A4'   C2 229C'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-107


                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  22A7'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  22A7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  22AA'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  22AB'   CD 13E7'              	CALL	CEND
  22AE'   D1                    	POP	D
  22AF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  22B2'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  22B3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  22B6'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  22B7'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  22B8'   FA 230A'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  22BB'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  22BC'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  22BD'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  22C0'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  22C3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  22C6'   DA 230A'              	JC	SUBL_D
  22C9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  22CC'   EB                    	XCHG
  22CD'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  22D0'   EB                    	XCHG
  22D1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  22D4'   EB                    	XCHG
  22D5'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  22D6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  22D9'   D1                    	POP	D
  22DA'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  22DB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  22DE'   3A 2768'              	LDA	SANY
  22E1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22E2'   C2 22EF'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  22E5'   3A 2769'              	LDA	SLCHG
  22E8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22E9'   C2 22EF'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  22EC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  22EF'                         SUBL_C:
  22EF'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  22F2'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  22F3'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  22F5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  22F8'   11 2772'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  22FB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  22FE'                         SUBL_I:
  22FE'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2300'   32 2769'              	STA	SLCHG
  2303'   3A 2767'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2306'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2307'   C2 22A7'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-108


  230A'                         SUBL_D:
  230A'   CD 2311'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  230D'   3A 2769'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2310'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2311'                         SUBBOL:
  2311'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2314'   CD 05CB'              	CALL	BOL
  2317'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  231A'                         VC_SRCH:
  231A'   CD 06DF'              	CALL	SETMOV
  231D'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  231E'   CD 0672'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2321'   22 275A'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2324'   C1                    	POP	B
  2325'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2326'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2328'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  232A'   CA 232F'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  232D'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  232F'                         VS_DIR:
  232F'   32 272F'              	STA	SDIR
  2332'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2335'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2338'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2339'   CD 1AD1'              	CALL	RDLINE
  233C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  233D'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  233E'   3A 2707'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2341'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2342'   CA 2357'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2345'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2346'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2348'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  234B'   19                    	DAD	D
  234C'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  234E'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  234F'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2352'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2354'   32 2731'              	STA	SHAVE
  2357'                         VS_OLD:
  2357'   3A 2731'              	LDA	SHAVE
  235A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  235B'   CA 2367'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  235E'   3A 272F'              	LDA	SDIR
  2361'   32 2730'              	STA	SDIRE
  2364'   C3 2390'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2367'                         VS_NOP:
  2367'   21 201F'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  236A'   C3 1F81'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-109


                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  236D'                         VC_N:
  236D'   CD 06DF'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2370'   3A 272F'              	LDA	SDIR
  2373'   C3 237D'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2376'                         VCB_N:
  2376'   CD 06DF'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2379'   3A 272F'              	LDA	SDIR
  237C'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  237D'                         VN_GO:
  237D'   32 2730'              	STA	SDIRE
  2380'   CD 0672'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2383'   22 275A'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2386'   3A 2731'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2389'   B7                    	ORA	A
  238A'   CA 2367'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  238D'   C3 2390'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2390'                         SRCHN:
  2390'   CD 0ED3'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2393'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2396'   22 275E'              	SHLD	SLDLT		; the sweep moves the cursor with PUTCUR, which
                                				;   does not maintain LNDLT (GOTO does)
  2399'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  239C'   22 275C'              	SHLD	SOFF
  239F'                         SN_LP:
  239F'   CD 23D7'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  23A2'   DA 23BC'              	JC	SN_BAD
  23A5'   2A 275A'              	LHLD	SCNT
  23A8'   2B                    	DCX	H
  23A9'   22 275A'              	SHLD	SCNT
  23AC'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  23AD'   B5                    	ORA	L
  23AE'   C2 239F'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  23B1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  23B4'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  23B6'   32 273C'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  23B9'   C3 0EEB'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  23BC'                         SN_BAD:
  23BC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  23BF'   2A 275C'              	LHLD	SOFF
  23C2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  23C5'   2A 275E'              	LHLD	SLDLT
  23C8'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  23CB'   21 200B'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  23CE'   CD 1F81'              	CALL	EXERR
  23D1'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  23D4'   C3 1F7C'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-110


                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  23D7'                         SRCHGO:
  23D7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  23D9'   32 2760'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  23DC'   CD 23EE'              	CALL	SRCHW
  23DF'   D0                    	RNC
  23E0'   3A 2730'              	LDA	SDIRE
  23E3'   2F                    	CMA
  23E4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  23E7'   AF                    	XRA	A
  23E8'   32 2760'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  23EB'   C3 23EE'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  23EE'                         SRCHW:
  23EE'   3A 2730'              	LDA	SDIRE
  23F1'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  23F4'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  23F7'   22 26AF'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  23FA'   AF                    	XRA	A
  23FB'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  23FE'                         SW_LP:
  23FE'   CD 240A'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2401'   D0                    	RNC
  2402'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2405'   D2 23FE'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2408'   37                    	STC
  2409'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  240A'                         SRCH1:
  240A'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  240D'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2410'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2411'   FA 2435'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2414'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2417'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  241A'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  241D'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  241E'   3A 2760'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2421'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2422'   CA 242B'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2425'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2426'   B1                    	ORA	C
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-111


  2427'   37                    	STC
  2428'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2429'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  242A'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  242B'                         SR1_FW:
  242B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  242E'   D8                    	RC
  242F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2432'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2435'                         SR1_BK:
  2435'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2438'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  243B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  243E'   D8                    	RC
  243F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2442'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2445'   D8                    	RC
  2446'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2449'                         MSGNAM:
  2449'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  244A'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  244C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  244F'   E1                    	POP	H
  2450'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2451'   23                    	INX	H
  2452'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2453'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2455'   C2 2462'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2458'   21 208D'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  245B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  245E'   E1                    	POP	H
  245F'   C3 24AC'              	JMP	MN_END
  2462'                         MN_HAS:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


  2462'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2464'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2467'   E1                    	POP	H
  2468'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2469'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  246A'   BE                    	CMP	M
  246B'   CA 2479'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  246E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  246F'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2471'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2474'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2476'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2479'                         MN_NAM:
  2479'   E1                    	POP	H
  247A'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  247B'   23                    	INX	H
  247C'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  247E'                         MN_NM:
  247E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  247F'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2481'   CA 248C'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2484'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2487'   23                    	INX	H
  2488'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2489'   C2 247E'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  248C'                         MN_EXT:
  248C'   E1                    	POP	H
  248D'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2490'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2491'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2492'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2494'   CA 24AC'              	JZ	MN_END
  2497'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2499'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  249C'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  249E'                         MN_XL:
  249E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  249F'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  24A1'   CA 24AC'              	JZ	MN_END
  24A4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  24A7'   23                    	INX	H
  24A8'   0D                    	DCR	C
  24A9'   C2 249E'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  24AC'                         MN_END:
  24AC'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  24AE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  24B1'                         MSGFLG:
  24B1'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  24B3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24B4'   CA 24C4'              	JZ	MF_RO
  24B7'   3A 26B2'              	LDA	MODF
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


  24BA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24BB'   CA 24C4'              	JZ	MF_RO
  24BE'   21 2097'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  24C1'   CD 24D9'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  24C4'                         MF_RO:
  24C4'   3A 271D'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  24C7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24C8'   CA 24D1'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  24CB'   21 20A2'              	LXI	H,SROM
  24CE'   CD 24D9'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  24D1'                         MF_NEW:
  24D1'   3A 271B'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  24D4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24D5'   C8                    	RZ
  24D6'   21 20AD'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  24D9'                         MF_PUT:
  24D9'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24DA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24DB'   C2 24E7'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  24DE'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  24E0'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24E1'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  24E3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  24E6'   E1                    	POP	H
  24E7'                         MF_STR:
  24E7'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  24EA'                         EMITW:
  24EA'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  24EB'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  24EC'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  24EE'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  24F1'   CD 250F'              	CALL	EWDIG
  24F4'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  24F7'   CD 250F'              	CALL	EWDIG
  24FA'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  24FD'   CD 250F'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2500'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2503'   CD 250F'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2506'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2507'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2509'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  250C'   D1                    	POP	D
  250D'   C1                    	POP	B
  250E'   C9                    	RET
  250F'                         EWDIG:
  250F'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2511'                         EW_LP:
  2511'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2512'   93                    	SUB	E
  2513'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2514'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2515'   9A                    	SBB	D
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  2516'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2517'   DA 251E'              	JC	EW_DN
  251A'   0C                    	INR	C
  251B'   C3 2511'              	JMP	EW_LP
  251E'                         EW_DN:
  251E'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  251F'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2520'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2521'   C2 2527'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2524'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2525'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2526'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2527'                         EW_PR:
  2527'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2528'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  252A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  252D'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  252F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2530'                         ISEMPT:
  2530'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2533'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2534'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2535'   C0                    	RNZ
  2536'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2539'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  253A'   B5                    	ORA	L
  253B'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  253C'                         MSGCTG:
  253C'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  253F'   CD 2449'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2542'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2544'   CD 24B1'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2547'   CD 2530'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  254A'   C2 2553'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  254D'   21 20C0'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2550'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2553'                         MG_POS:
  2553'   21 20B3'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2556'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2559'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  255C'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  255D'   CD 24EA'              	CALL	EMITW
  2560'   21 20BA'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2563'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2566'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2569'   23                    	INX	H
  256A'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  256B'   CD 24EA'              	CALL	EMITW
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  256E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2571'   23                    	INX	H
  2572'   D1                    	POP	D
  2573'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2576'   C8                    	RZ
  2577'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2578'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  257A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  257D'   E1                    	POP	H
  257E'   C3 24EA'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2581'                         FILMSG:
  2581'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2584'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2586'   C8                    	RZ
  2587'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  258A'   CD 2449'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  258D'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  258E'   C3 24B1'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2591'                         WRMSG:
  2591'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2594'   CD 2449'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2597'   21 20D2'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  259A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  259D'                         MSGPST:
  259D'   3A 271A'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  25A0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25A1'   C8                    	RZ
  25A2'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  25A3'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25A4'   32 271A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  25A7'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  25A8'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  25AA'   CA 25C6'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  25AD'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  25AF'   CA 25CC'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  25B2'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  25B4'   CA 25BD'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  25B7'   CD 253C'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  25BA'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  25BD'                         MP_BIG:
  25BD'   21 206E'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  25C0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  25C3'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  25C6'                         MP_FIL:
  25C6'   CD 2581'              	CALL	FILMSG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25C9'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  25CC'                         MP_WRT:
  25CC'   CD 2591'              	CALL	WRMSG
  25CF'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  25D2'                         VC_CTLG:
  25D2'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  25D4'   32 271A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  25D7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  25D9'   32 26B3'              	STA	RINTENT
  25DC'   C9                    	RET
                                
  25DD'                         CMDTAB:
  25DD'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  25DE'   25D2'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  25E0'   68                    	DB	'h'
  25E1'   0738'                 	DW	VC_H
  25E3'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  25E4'   0762'                 	DW	VC_L
  25E6'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  25E7'   0827'                 	DW	VC_J
  25E9'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  25EA'   0836'                 	DW	VC_K
  25EC'   30                    	DB	'0'
  25ED'   0789'                 	DW	VC_0
  25EF'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  25F0'   07A7'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  25F2'   24                    	DB	'$'
  25F3'   07E5'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  25F5'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  25F6'   0809'                 	DW	VC_CR
  25F8'   77                    	DB	'w'
  25F9'   0A0B'                 	DW	VC_W
  25FB'   62                    	DB	'b'
  25FC'   0A5C'                 	DW	VC_B
  25FE'   65                    	DB	'e'
  25FF'   0BC1'                 	DW	VC_E
  2601'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2602'   0A12'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2604'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2605'   0A63'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2607'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2608'   0BC8'                 	DW	VCBGE
  260A'   47                    	DB	'G'
  260B'   0E23'                 	DW	VC_G
  260D'   67                    	DB	'g'
  260E'   0E67'                 	DW	VC_LG
